<header>
  <div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div>
  <h1>What Do Businesses Actually Deal In?</h1>
  <div class="sub">Week 2 · Day 1 — Unit IV: Economics</div>
</header>

<div class="timestrip">
  <div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div>
  <div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Opening Hook</div>
  <div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>DI Instruction</div>
  <div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Practice / SPED Pull-Aside</div>
  <div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Logoff</div>
</div>

<div class="wrap">

<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  Introduces the vocabulary this whole week runs on (business, goods, services, consumer, profit, resources) and opens the two BMG blocks Week 2 builds: Revenue Streams (what a business earns) and Cost Structure (what it costs to provide). Backwards Design goal: students apply scarcity, resource allocation, and competition to real spending decisions.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Opening Hook</h2>

<div class="note iefa">
  <span class="label">IEFA Connection</span>
  Resource scarcity is not a new idea on the reservation — decisions about limited land, water, and seasonal resources have always required real economic reasoning, not textbook theory. Set this up now; it becomes the anchor for Day 3's fluency work.
</div>

<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Quick question before we define anything: name one thing your family has had to make a choice about because there wasn't enough of it — time, money, water, land, anything.</p>
  <p class="a">Take 3–4 shares. Don't over-explain yet — just collect real answers.</p>
  <p class="t">Every one of those is an example of <span class="k">limited resources</span> — and managing limited resources well isn't new to this community. It's something Blackfeet families have done with land, water, and seasonal resources for generations. Businesses run into the exact same problem. Today we get the vocabulary for it.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — I Do / We Do / You Do</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — Core Vocabulary</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">A <span class="k">business</span> sells <span class="k">goods</span> — things you can hold — or <span class="k">services</span> — things someone does for you. The person who buys them is the <span class="k">consumer</span>. What's left over after a business pays its costs is <span class="k">profit</span>. And <span class="k">economics</span> is just the study of how people make decisions with limited resources.</p>
  <p class="t">Say it with me:</p>
  <p class="cr">"Goods you hold. Services someone does."</p>
  <p class="t">There are three kinds of <span class="k">resources</span> every business needs: natural resources, human resources — that's people and labor — and capital resources, meaning money and equipment. All three are <span class="k">limited</span>. That's the whole problem economics exists to solve.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">We Do — Wants vs. Needs Sort</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">I'll name something. Thumbs up if it's a need, thumbs down if it's a want.</p>
  <p class="a">Call out: water, a new phone, food, name-brand shoes, shelter, video games, medicine, a second car. Discuss any split votes — needs vs. wants gets genuinely blurry for some of these, and that's the point.</p>
  <p class="t">Businesses have to fulfill needs first — because of limited resources, they can't do everything. Only once needs are covered does chasing wants make sense.</p>
</div>

<div class="note channel">
  <span class="label">Learning Channel</span>
  Hear/Do (vocabulary chorus) into See/Say (thumbs sort) — keeps this from becoming a lecture-only day even though it's vocabulary-heavy.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">You Do — Guided Practice</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">In your workbook — for each business listed, mark whether it fulfills a want or a need, and whether it sells a good or a service. Some might do both.</p>
  <p class="a">List: Spa, Buffalo Wild Wings, Roofing Company, Goodwill, Movie Theater, Dillons, Cell Phone Repair Store, Pet Grooming Store, Hospital. Circulate — expect real debate on several of these (is a movie theater a want or... entertainment as a need for mental health? Let students argue it out.)</p>
</div>

<div class="note comprehension">
  <span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>
  This matrix is where students either genuinely reason through the want/need distinction or just guess quickly to finish. Cold-call a few "why" questions on the debatable ones (Goodwill, Movie Theater) rather than just checking for completion.
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Practice — Two Tracks Running Concurrently</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Independent Practice</h3>
<p>Students finish the business matrix, then answer: "What do businesses try to fulfill for consumers?" and "What are the three resources?" in their workbook — direct application of today's I Do content.</p>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "What I Earn" (Revenue, Simple Money Math)</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">A business sells things to earn money. That money is called revenue. Let's practice — if you sell one bracelet for $5, how much money do you earn?</p>
    <p class="s">[$5]</p>
    <p class="t">If you sell two bracelets for $5 each, how much?</p>
    <p class="a">Use physical play money and real objects (or pictures of objects) — keep numbers small and concrete, build up one bracelet at a time rather than jumping to abstract totals.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="note autism" style="margin-top:14px;">
    <span class="label">Autism Observation Tip</span>
    If a student wants to know exactly why $5 and not another number, that's a fair question — give a simple, real reason ("that's what people are willing to pay for a bracelet like this") rather than "just because."
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">One sentence: what's one thing a business has to fulfill first — a want, or a need — and why?</p>
  <p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent per student.</p>
</div>

<table class="assess">
  <tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="identity">
  <h3>Managing Limited Resources Is Already Familiar</h3>
  <p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. The choices you named at the start of class — about time, water, land, money — those weren't hypothetical. That's real economic reasoning, and you were already doing it before we put a name on it today.</p>
</div>

</div>
</body>
