<header>
  <div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div>
  <h1>The $500 Problem</h1>
  <div class="sub">Week 2 · Day 2 — Unit IV: Economics — Limited Resources in Practice</div>
</header>

<div class="timestrip">
  <div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div>
  <div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Choral Recall</div>
  <div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>DI Instruction</div>
  <div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Budget Decision / SPED Pull-Aside</div>
  <div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Logoff</div>
</div>

<div class="wrap">

<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  Direct application of yesterday's vocabulary — students apply scarcity and resource allocation to one concrete decision, not abstract definitions.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Yesterday — goods are...</p>
  <p class="cr">"Things you hold."</p>
  <p class="t">Services are...</p>
  <p class="cr">"Things someone does."</p>
  <p class="t">And businesses have to fulfill what first — wants, or needs?</p>
  <p class="cr">"Needs."</p>
  <p class="t">Today you're the one making that call — with real numbers.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — The Budget Scenario</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — Setting the Scenario</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">You're the manager of a burger fast-food joint. You're running low on hamburger patties. You'd also like to get your employees new uniforms. You have exactly $500 left in your monthly budget — not a dollar more. What do you spend it on?</p>
  <p class="a">Let the tension sit for a second before moving on — don't rush to resolve it.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">We Do — Working Through the Trade-Off</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Which one is a need for this business to keep running — patties, or uniforms?</p>
  <p class="s">[patties]</p>
  <p class="t">Right — without patties, you can't sell burgers at all. That's <span class="k">scarcity</span> forcing a real decision. Uniforms matter, but they're not stopping the business from operating today. This is what economists call <span class="k">opportunity cost</span> — say it with me:</p>
  <p class="cr">"What you give up to get something else."</p>
  <p class="t">The opportunity cost of buying patties is... the new uniforms, at least for now.</p>
</div>

<div class="note channel">
  <span class="label">Learning Channel</span>
  Think/Say — this scenario is deliberately uncomfortable (there's a real cost to the "right" answer) so opportunity cost isn't just a vocabulary word, it's a felt trade-off.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">You Do — Guided Practice</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">In your workbook: why is competition good for businesses and consumers? And then — true or false — work through the statement list.</p>
  <p class="a">True/False set: businesses decide what to provide based on what you want and need; your wants never change; the motive of business is to make profit and add value to society; businesses help the economy by providing jobs; it's important to make good decisions in business; you're a consumer if you purchase and use a product/service; a company can survive without profit; businesses are hurt by competition.</p>
</div>

<div class="note comprehension">
  <span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>
  "A company can survive without profit" and "your wants never change" are the two most likely to get a rushed guess. Push for the reasoning on both — these are the statements that actually test understanding, not recall.
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Practice — Two Tracks Running Concurrently</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Independent Practice</h3>
<p>Finish the true/false set. Early finishers: think about a time your own family had to choose between two needs at once — what did you pick, and what was the opportunity cost? (No submission required — oral rehearsal for Day 4.)</p>

<div class="choice-moment">
  <span class="cm-label">Choice Moment</span>
  If you were the manager, would you split the $500 unevenly instead of picking just one — and if so, how? Genuinely open; there's no single correct split, only a defensible one.
</div>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "Only Enough for One" (Simple Scarcity)</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">You have enough money for one thing. A snack, or a toy. Which do you need more today?</p>
    <p class="a">Use two picture cards (food, toy) and real or play money in a small, fixed amount. Repeat with a second pair (water bottle vs. sticker) to build the pattern before moving to the business framing.</p>
    <p class="t">A business has the same problem. Only enough money for one thing. They have to pick the need first.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">One sentence: if you only had enough money for one thing, how do you decide which one to pick?</p>
  <p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent per student.</p>
</div>

<table class="assess">
  <tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="identity">
  <h3>Choosing Well Under Pressure Is a Skill</h3>
  <p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. Today's decision wasn't easy on purpose. Real business owners face exactly this kind of pressure — and so does anyone managing a household with limited resources. You already know how to do this.</p>
</div>

</div>
