<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  Precision Teaching day — no new content, fluency (accuracy + speed) on Week 2 vocabulary: business, goods, services, consumer, profit, resources, scarcity, opportunity cost. Anchored in a real, local example so "scarcity" isn't abstract, the same way Week 1's Day 3 used dentalium for "value."
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Quick fire. Scarcity means...</p>
  <p class="cr">"Not enough of something to go around."</p>
  <p class="t">Opportunity cost means...</p>
  <p class="cr">"What you give up to get something else."</p>
  <p class="t">Today, no new ideas — just getting fast and accurate.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · Stewardship Mini-Lesson + Fluency Rounds</h2>

<div class="note iefa">
  <span class="label">IEFA Connection — Resource Stewardship on the Reservation</span>
  Managing limited land, water, and seasonal resources has always required real economic decision-making within the Blackfeet Nation — this is not a hypothetical exercise for this community, it's lived practice. Keep specifics general at this stage; verify any current, specific claims (water rights status, particular land-use policy details) with a community or tribal source before stating them as fact in the final script — I have not independently verified those specifics.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — Scarcity Isn't New Here</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">This whole week we've been talking about scarcity like it's a business school idea. It's not new. Blackfeet families and the Nation as a whole have managed limited land, water, and seasonal resources for generations — deciding what to use now, what to hold back, and how to make what's limited last.</p>
  <p class="t">That's the same skill as the $500 problem from yesterday. Different scale, same reasoning: what matters most right now, with what we actually have.</p>
  <p class="a">If you have a specific, verified local or family example you're comfortable sharing (seasonal resource decisions, land use, water), this is the moment for it — a real family or community example will land far better than a general statement.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">Precision Teaching — Three-Round Fluency Structure</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="a"><span class="k">Round 1 — With Support:</span> Word wall visible (business, goods, services, consumer, profit, resources, scarcity, opportunity cost). Call a term, students respond chorally, checking wall as needed.</p>
  <p class="a"><span class="k">Round 2 — Without Support:</span> Cover the word wall. Timed — 60 seconds. Call terms rapid-fire, individual students respond, log speed and accuracy.</p>
</div>

<div class="fluency-table">
  <tr><th>Round</th><th>Support</th><th>Timing</th><th>Response Mode</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>Word wall visible</td><td>Untimed</td><td>Choral</td></tr>
  <tr><td>2</td><td>None</td><td>60 sec, timed</td><td>Individual, cold-call</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3</td><td>None</td><td>Untimed</td><td>Peer partner quiz</td></tr>
</div>

<div class="note adhd">
  <span class="label">ADHD Observation Tip</span>
  Same as Week 1 Day 3 — the timer in Round 2 can spike blurting. Keep the visible "hands down, wait for your name" cue consistent across both fluency days so it's a familiar routine by now, not a new rule to relearn.
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Round 3 — Peer Identification / SPED Pull-Aside</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Round 3</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Partner up, quiz each other on all eight terms. If your partner's stuck, ask a question that helps them find the answer — don't just give it to them.</p>
  <p class="a">Circulate and log Not Yet/Developing/Fluent per student based on Rounds 2 and 3 combined.</p>
</div>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "Enough for Now" (Stewardship, Concrete)</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level. Untimed, repeated practice reps.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">Some things run out if you use too much too fast — water, food, time. What is something that can run out if you're not careful?</p>
    <p class="s">[names something]</p>
    <p class="t">Taking care of something so it lasts is called stewardship. Families here have done that with land and water for a long time.</p>
    <p class="a">Return to Day 1's $5-bracelet math — connect: "If you only had a few bracelets to sell, would you sell them all at once, or save some for later?" Reinforces limited-resource reasoning without new vocabulary load.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Fast round, no writing: I say a term, you say the definition.</p>
</div>

<table class="assess">
  <tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="identity">
  <h3>Stewardship Is Economic Reasoning</h3>
  <p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. Managing what's limited isn't a school subject you're learning from scratch. It's something this community has practiced for generations. Tomorrow you start your own research — looking at how businesses actually price things.</p>
</div>

