</div>

<div class="wrap">

<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  This launches the Week 2 end-of-week artifact — completed price-comparison research, which feeds Week 6's pricing decisions. Today students choose an item and begin the research; Day 5 finalizes it.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">This week — scarcity means...</p>
  <p class="cr">"Not enough of something to go around."</p>
  <p class="t">Today we ask a real question: does the exact same item cost the exact same amount everywhere?</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — Why Prices Differ</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — Setting the Task</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Think of one item you actually love — something you'd buy without thinking twice. You're going to go find it online, at three different sites, and write down the price at each one.</p>
  <p class="a">Model your own example quickly on the board — search one product, show the first result, model writing down the price and link.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">We Do — Predicting Why Prices Differ</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Before you search — guess with me. Why might the exact same item cost different amounts at different stores?</p>
  <p class="a">Take several guesses, write on board without confirming yet: shipping costs, store overhead, competition, brand reputation, sales/discounts, bulk buying power.</p>
  <p class="t">All of those are real reasons. This connects straight back to <span class="k">competition</span> — when businesses compete for the same customer, price is one of the biggest ways they compete.</p>
</div>

<div class="note channel">
  <span class="label">Learning Channel</span>
  Think/Say (prediction) before Think/Do (research) — students commit to a guess before they search, so the research actually tests their thinking instead of being a blind data-collection task.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">You Do — Begin Research</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Find your item, then find the exact same item on two other sites. Write down the price and the link for all three.</p>
  <p class="a">Since AI tools are blocked district-wide, this is straightforward web search on approved sites — model the search process once if students are unfamiliar with comparing listings.</p>
</div>

<div class="note adhd">
  <span class="label">ADHD Observation Tip</span>
  Open-ended web research is a common derailment point — the freedom to pick "anything you love" can spiral into browsing unrelated items. A visible timer for the research block and a check-in halfway through helps more than repeated verbal redirects.
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Research Time — Two Tracks Running Concurrently</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Independent Research</h3>
<p>Students complete the three-site price search. Early finishers begin drafting: are the prices different? If yes, why do they think so (at least two paragraphs' worth of reasoning by tomorrow). If no, why do they think the prices matched exactly?</p>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "Same Thing, Different Price?"</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">I found the same snack at two stores. Look — one costs more than the other. Why do you think that happens?</p>
    <p class="a">Bring two real or printed examples of the same simple item (a candy bar, a drink) at two different visible prices — physical props work better than abstract discussion here.</p>
    <p class="s">[guesses — one store is bigger, one is closer, etc.]</p>
    <p class="t">You're right — stores make different choices, and that changes the price.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">One sentence: what item did you pick, and were the three prices the same or different?</p>
  <p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent per student.</p>
</div>

