<header>
  <div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div>
  <h1>Finishing the Research</h1>
  <div class="sub">Week 2 · Day 5 — Portfolio Piece, Preview of Week 3</div>
</header>

<div class="timestrip">
  <div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div>
  <div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Week 2 Recap</div>
  <div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>Finish Research Write-Up</div>
  <div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Share Out / SPED Pull-Aside</div>
  <div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Preview Week 3</div>
</div>

<div class="wrap">

<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  Finalizes the Week 2 end-of-week artifact — completed price-comparison research — for the running Week 1–9 workbook section. Feeds directly into Week 6's pricing decisions.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Week 2 Recap</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">This week: scarcity means?</p>
  <p class="cr">"Not enough of something to go around."</p>
  <p class="t">Opportunity cost means?</p>
  <p class="cr">"What you give up to get something else."</p>
  <p class="t">And businesses have to fulfill what first?</p>
  <p class="cr">"Needs."</p>
  <p class="t">Today we finish proving all three ideas with real research you did yourself.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — Finishing the Write-Up</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — What a Strong Explanation Looks Like</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Yesterday you predicted why prices might differ, then you researched. Today you finish two paragraphs: if the prices were different, explain why, using what we've learned this week — scarcity, competition, resources. If the prices were exactly the same, explain why you think that happened instead.</p>
  <p class="a">Model turning a vague guess ("maybe shipping") into a specific claim tied to vocabulary: "The price is likely higher on Site A because of shipping costs built into the price — that's part of their cost structure, which affects what they need to charge to still make a profit."</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">We Do — Strengthening Weak Explanations</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Read this out loud with me — is this a strong explanation or a weak one? "The prices were different because stores are different."</p>
  <p class="s">[weak — too vague]</p>
  <p class="t">Right. What's missing?</p>
  <p class="s">[a specific reason — which term from this week explains it]</p>
</div>

<div class="note comprehension">
  <span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>
  This is the moment students either connect the week's vocabulary to their own research or write a generic paragraph that could apply to anything. Circulate specifically checking whether their explanation uses at least one term from this week (scarcity, competition, cost structure, resources) — not just checking for two paragraphs of length.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">You Do — Final Write</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Finish your explanation. This is the version that goes in your portfolio and comes back in Week 6 when you price your own idea.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Voluntary Share-Out / Two Tracks</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Optional Share</h3>
<p>Ask for 2–3 volunteers to share their item and their explanation. Look for variety in reasoning across shares if possible.</p>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — Finishing the Week</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">This week you learned: things can run out, and the same thing can cost different amounts. Let's put it in one sentence — I looked at [item], and it cost different amounts because [reason].</p>
    <p class="a">Help each student assemble their own sentence orally, then support them writing or dictating it into the workbook.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check + Preview Week 3</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Before you log off — next week we stop looking at prices and start looking for opportunities. We're going to find a real need in this community that nobody's fully solved yet. Come back thinking about something that bugs you or someone you know — something that could use a better solution.</p>
  <p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent — final Week 2 mark, based on the finished artifact's use of vocabulary and reasoning quality.</p>
</div>

<table class="assess">
  <tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="identity">
  <h3>You Just Did Real Market Research</h3>
  <p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. What you did this week wasn't a worksheet exercise — you researched, predicted, tested your prediction, and explained it with real reasoning. That's the actual work of running a business. It stays in your workbook, and it comes back when you price your own idea in a few weeks.</p>
</div>

